{:title
 "The Smile Editor, From the Smile Composer GUI to an Editor That Knows the Faces",
 :description
 "What the editor is in THE MAP of Smile, an editor that reads the version and defaults in use and changes a face with all its matched faces in one edit, and how my words reached it, from the keyboard-driven Smile Composer of October and November 2025 to the editor of August 2026, with every source.",
 :keywords
 "Smile editor, Smile Composer GUI, prompt editor interface, keyboard navigation prompt editor, modular prompt editor, Smile prompt language, history of Smile",
 :lede
 "In THE MAP, the editor reads the version and the defaults in use, such as the five-tier hierarchy, four-space indentation and the header as a face, a label and a face. Changing one face updates its matched faces, so the four faces of a section change in one edit.",
 :paras
 ["THE MAP lets an edit reach below the face, to the mouth or the nose alone. The part is its own piece, so changing it recomposes every face gathering it and their paired closers with them. The reach of a part edit is set as everywhere else in Smile, changed at the part's tier for every face, or kept at the face's tier as an override of that one face. Writing at any tier is assisted by what the version binds, such as the dressing for the depth being written at."
 "The Smile Composer was built with ChatGPT and Claude from 31 October 2025, a tree of agents, modules and instructions moved through by keyboard. “This is literally just a bullet point list where when you click on one item in that bullet point list it makes another bullet point list belowo it of items and that keeps going until we get to actual text.”, and “the visuals need to be spacious, delightful.”[[m:2025-10-31#3]] On 1 November, “We are always retaining both keyboard and mouse interactions.”[[m:2025-11-01]] On 2 November, “structure must support infinite recursiveness with increased tabbing.”[[m:2025-11-02]] On 3 November, “we need everything to be in fidelity with a core vision - anything opened on a hierarchy lower is tabbed inside one tab amount.”[[m:2025-11-03]] The same day, “The app is still called (: Smile Composer.”[[m:2025-11-03#2]]"
 "On 6 November I asked that the composer read and write files, “This needs to all be 1:1 mirrored with the prompt directory.”,[[m:2025-11-06]] and I wrote of it, “I've also created a GUI so I can edit modules (sets of composed instructions that do similar roles) and instructions (semantically unique instructions separated into files).”[[m:2025-11-06#2]] On 9 November, “Press enter at any time, on any module, agent or instruction, from any view or level to see the full prompt compiled for that module, agent ,instruction, etc.”[[m:2025-11-09#3]]"
 "In late November the composer became two panels, a hierarchy tree on the left and a text editor on the right. “The right panel is full of textareas that expand on click.”[[m:2025-11-13]] On 20 November, “you must be able to parse .smile files back into the interface they were created from, as users can share these files with each other to share prompts, modules etc.”[[m:2025-11-20]] On 22 November, “It must read like one long text document”, “The right panel is basically a text editor.”, and “Focus the app on operating extremely quickly.”[[m:2025-11-22#2]] In December the Smile GUI prompt gathered the specification.[[m:2025-12-02]]"
 "Through early 2026 the GUI waited, “I also have the GUI partially built.”,[[m:2026-02-20]] and its aim widened, “what I basically want is for the interface also to allow prompt engineering exploration and ideation beyond a chat window.”[[m:2026-01-27]]"
 "In August 2026 I asked for the editor THE MAP describes. On 11 August I asked for compositions I could read, “its meant to be so i can read and edit compositions clearly because i cant read TOML”.[[m:2026-08-11]] On 12 August, “We will also have an editor where e.g. you have set your preferences for the Smile hierarchy format (e.g. the 5 tier hierarchy) and it is aware of the smile default you are using so it knows to use 4 spaces”.[[m:2026-08-12]]"
 "So the editor moved from a keyboard tree of modules and instructions in 2025, through a two panel text editor, to an editor asked to know the version's faces and change their pairs together, part built, across {{moments}} moments and {{quotes}} quotes from {{first}} to {{last}}. Editing a render directly is followed in [[t:composing-from-inside-a-prompt]], and the tiers an edit reaches in [[t:magnification]]."],
 :faq
